# Grade 11 (part d): teaching scripts, student steps and saint stories.
R = {
'11-9-1': dict(
  saint="Rita of Cascia: a wife in a violent marriage, a mother, a widow and a sister, who prayed for peace.",
  tryit=["Distinguish annulment from divorce.", "List grounds for nullity.", "Describe the Church’s care for the divorced."],
  saintStory="Rita was born around 1381 in Roccaporena, Italy. She wished to be a nun, but her parents arranged a marriage at twelve to Paolo, a man with a violent temper. For about eighteen years, she endured his anger and infidelity. She prayed for him and treated him with patience, and eventually he changed. Not long afterward, he was killed in a feud.\nHer twin sons wanted revenge for their father’s death. Rita begged them to forgive. Fearful that they would commit murder, she prayed that God would take them before they sinned. Both sons died of illness within a year, after having reconciled.\nWidowed and childless, Rita sought to join the Augustinian convent in Cascia, and was refused three times, because of the feuds in her family. She helped reconcile the families, and was admitted. She lived there forty years in prayer. A wound appeared on her forehead, from a thorn of Christ’s crown, as she prayed before a crucifix. She died in 1457. She is called the “Saint of the Impossible” and is invoked by those in difficult marriages.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say softly, “Many of you have family or friends affected by divorce. This is a subject that requires tenderness. We will state the Church’s teaching clearly, and also the Church’s care.” Ask, “What do you think the Church should say to a person whose marriage has ended?”",
    teach=["Read Mark 10:2-12 and Matthew 19:3-9. Say: “The Pharisees asked about divorce. Jesus pointed back to Genesis: ‘From the beginning of creation, God made them male and female… What therefore God has joined together, let not man put asunder.’ He said that Moses allowed divorce because of hardness of heart, but ‘it was not so from the beginning.’ He taught that whoever divorces and remarries commits adultery. The Church holds that a valid, consummated sacramental marriage is indissoluble; it can be ended only by death. No power on earth, not even the Pope, can dissolve it.”", "Say: “Annulment. An annulment (a declaration of nullity) is not a ‘Catholic divorce.’ It is a finding by a Church tribunal that, despite appearances, a valid marriage never existed from the beginning, because something essential was lacking at the time of the wedding. Grounds include: lack of canonical form; an impediment (such as a prior bond); lack of freedom (force or grave fear); lack of due discretion or the capacity to assume the obligations of marriage; deceit; simulation (excluding permanence, fidelity or children); condition. The process: petition to the diocesan tribunal, testimony, witnesses, review by a judge, and a defender of the bond. Pope Francis streamlined it in 2015 (Mitis Iudex). Children from an annulled marriage remain legitimate. The process can be difficult but can bring healing.”", "Say: “Practical teaching. Civil divorce is sometimes tolerated as the only way to protect rights or children (CCC 2383). Separation without remarriage is allowed in cases of abuse or danger; Rita’s situation shows a dangerous marriage, and the Church does not require anyone to remain in danger. Divorced Catholics are not excommunicated and remain members. Those who have remarried without an annulment cannot receive Communion, since their situation contradicts the union of Christ and the Church, but are encouraged to attend Mass, pray, and take part in the community. Pope Francis in Amoris Laetitia (2016) asked pastors to accompany them, discern, and integrate them. Children of divorce are not at fault. The Church ministers through programs like Rachel’s Vineyard and divorce support groups. Prevention: good preparation for marriage, and support afterward.”"],
    ask=["Can the Church dissolve a valid sacramental marriage? (No.)", "What is an annulment? (A declaration that a valid marriage never existed.)", "May a divorced Catholic receive Communion? (Yes, unless remarried without annulment.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart: divorce vs annulment (what it is, who decides, effects).", "List the main grounds for nullity.", "Write a short compassionate message you would send to a friend whose parents are divorcing.", "Identify one resource at your parish for those in difficult marriages."],
    close="Pray for families in pain. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for a family that is hurting.”")),

'11-9-2': dict(
  saint="Aelred of Rievaulx: an abbot who wrote about holy friendship.",
  tryit=["State the Church’s teaching in her own words.", "Identify the three requirements of pastoral care.", "Draft a respectful response to a friend."],
  saintStory="Aelred was born around 1110 in northern England, to a family of priests. He grew up at the court of King David of Scotland, as a steward. He became a Cistercian monk at Rievaulx in 1134, and later abbot in 1147, leading a community of about 600 monks and lay brothers.\nAelred wrote Spiritual Friendship, based on Cicero’s dialogue On Friendship, but transformed by Christ. In it, he taught that “God is friendship.” He wrote of friendship as a path to God, grounded in a shared love of Christ. Friends aid each other in virtue and share a love of what is good. He said, “Here we are, you and I, and I hope a third, Christ, is in our midst.”\nHe had been tender and affectionate with his monks. He suffered from painful illness in his last years. He died in 1167. His writings show that deep friendship is a gift in the celibate life.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say gently, “This lesson deals with a sensitive subject. In this room, everyone is to be treated with respect, whatever their experience. We will state the Church’s teaching carefully, with its call to love, and give time for questions.” Ask, “What is the difference between how the Church treats a person and what the Church teaches about acts?”",
    teach=["Say: “The Catechism teaches (CCC 2357-2359): ‘Homosexuality refers to relations between men or between women who experience an exclusive or predominant sexual attraction toward persons of the same sex.’ Tradition, based on Scripture (Genesis 19, Romans 1:24-27, 1 Corinthians 6:9-10), has always declared that ‘homosexual acts are intrinsically disordered’ and contrary to the natural law, since they close the sexual act to the gift of life and do not proceed from a genuine complementarity. The Church distinguishes between the orientation (an inclination, not a sin) and acts. ‘The number of men and women who have deep-seated homosexual tendencies is not negligible. This inclination, which is objectively disordered, constitutes for most of them a trial.’”", "Say: “Then, crucially: ‘They must be accepted with respect, compassion, and sensitivity. Every sign of unjust discrimination in their regard should be avoided.’ (CCC 2358) ‘Homosexual persons are called to chastity.’ Like all, they’re called to self-mastery, to friendship, to prayer, to the sacraments, and to Christian perfection. Pope Francis asked, ‘Who am I to judge?’ about a gay person who seeks the Lord with goodwill, and has also said the Church should welcome. The Church rejects violence and harassment against gay persons, and it’s a serious sin to treat them with contempt. Bullying is wrong. Parents should love their children whatever. The Church says that ‘God’s love is for everyone.’”", "Say: “Questions arise. Why isn’t same-sex marriage recognized? Because the Church sees marriage as the union of a man and a woman, ordered to the good of the spouses and children (the complementarity of the sexes). Civil law is different from the sacrament, and the Church has asked that civil law not redefine marriage. The Church recognizes that people disagree, including many Catholics. Experiences vary: some people find the teaching hard and feel excluded. Others live it with joy: groups such as Courage offer support for chaste living. The Church acknowledges failures in the past: too often, gay people were met with cruelty. Our call: truth and love together. Practical guidance: listen, don’t assume, don’t use slurs, keep friendships, point to Christ, avoid both condemnation and dismissal of the teaching. Aelred’s ideal of friendship in Christ is available to all.”"],
    ask=["Is a homosexual orientation a sin? (No; the Church distinguishes orientation from acts.)", "How should persons with same-sex attraction be treated? (With respect, compassion and sensitivity; no unjust discrimination.)", "To what are they called? (Chastity, friendship, prayer, sacraments, holiness.)"],
    practice=["Write the Church’s teaching in four sentences, including both the moral teaching and the call to respect.", "Draft a respectful response to a friend who says the Church hates gay people.", "List what a welcoming parish does and does not do.", "Share with a partner."],
    close="Pray: “Lord, help us to love everyone with truth and tenderness.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Treat everyone you meet with dignity.”")),

'11-9-3': dict(
  saint="Cicely Saunders: a nurse and doctor who founded modern hospice care.",
  tryit=["Distinguish killing from allowing to die.", "Distinguish ordinary from extraordinary means.", "Describe good palliative care."],
  saintStory="Cicely Saunders was born in 1918 in England. She trained as a nurse, then a social worker, and finally became a doctor at thirty-three. In 1948, she befriended a dying patient, David Tasma, a Polish Jewish refugee. He asked her, “I only want what is in your mind and in your heart.” He left her £500, saying, “I will be a window in your home.”\nShe spent years working in hospitals for the dying. She saw that patients suffered from physical pain but also from fear, loneliness and the sense of being a burden. She developed the idea of “total pain”: physical, emotional, social and spiritual. She taught regular doses of pain relief to prevent pain, rather than waiting for it to return.\nIn 1967 she opened St. Christopher’s Hospice in London, the first modern hospice. It became a model across the world. She said to patients, “You matter because you are you, and you matter to the end of your life. We will do all we can not only to help you die peacefully, but also to live until you die.” She was a devout Christian. She died in 2005 in her own hospice.",
  script=dict(
    open="Be calm. Say, “This is a hard topic. Some of you may have lost someone or face illness in your family. We’ll treat it carefully.” Ask, “What matters most to a person near the end of life?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “The Church teaches that life is a gift, and we are stewards, not owners. The Catechism: ‘Euthanasia, an action or omission which of itself or by intention causes death in order that all suffering may be eliminated, is morally unacceptable.’ (CCC 2277) Euthanasia is the intentional killing of a sick or dying person. Assisted suicide is helping someone to kill themselves. Both are wrong, even with consent and even for compassion. Suicide is gravely contrary to love of self, God and neighbor. The Church recognizes that psychological disturbance, anguish or fear can diminish responsibility (CCC 2282-2283); we do not despair of the salvation of those who take their own lives, and pray for them.”", "Say: “Distinctions. The Church allows, and sometimes requires, the withdrawal or refusal of treatment that is burdensome, dangerous, extraordinary or disproportionate to the expected outcome. This is not euthanasia, but acceptance of the human condition. Ordinary means, such as food, water, and basic care, must be provided unless they cannot be absorbed or cause grave burden. Pain relief, even if it might shorten life as an unintended effect, is allowed. This is the principle of double effect. Palliative care, which treats pain and symptoms, supports the whole person. Hospice affirms that dying is a natural process. Advance directives and a health care proxy are advisable, and should be consistent with Church teaching.”", "Say: “Why not euthanasia? Because it treats a person’s worth as dependent on health or usefulness; because it can become pressure on the vulnerable; because it changes the practice of medicine from healing to killing; and because suffering has meaning when united with love, though we should work to relieve it. Pope John Paul II’s Evangelium Vitae: ‘Euthanasia is a grave violation of the law of God.’ Pope Francis: ‘We must accompany people towards death, but not provoke death.’ Countries that have legalized euthanasia have seen the categories expand. Care for the dying is an act of love: presence, touch, prayer, sacraments (Anointing, Viaticum), forgiveness, farewells. Saunders’s total pain approach cares for all dimensions. As the Church says, ‘Death is not the end but the doorway.’ Hope in resurrection changes how we die.”"],
    ask=["Is euthanasia acceptable? (No; the Church teaches it is morally wrong.)", "May we refuse burdensome treatment? (Yes, extraordinary or disproportionate means may be refused.)", "What is palliative care? (Care that relieves pain and supports the whole person.)"],
    practice=["Prepare a chart: euthanasia, refusing extraordinary treatment, palliative sedation, and hospice. Mark which are allowed and which not, and why.", "Write a short explanation of ‘total pain.’", "Draft a letter to a dying relative, expressing love and gratitude.", "List ways to accompany the dying."],
    close="Pray for the dying: “Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord.” Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Ask a trusted adult about your family’s wishes for care.”")),

'11-10-1': dict(
  saint="Simon Stock: a Carmelite associated by tradition with the brown scapular.",
  tryit=["Sort items into sacraments, sacramentals and devotions.", "Say what each does.", "Explain how to use them rightly."],
  saintStory="Simon Stock is a figure whose life is mostly known through legend. He was an Englishman who is said to have lived as a hermit in the hollow of a tree (hence “Stock”), before joining the Carmelites, who had been driven out of the Holy Land. He became their superior general around 1247 and helped adapt their rule for a life in Europe.\nAccording to tradition, on July 16, 1251, in Cambridge, he was praying to Mary for help for his order, which was in difficulty. She appeared to him holding the brown scapular, a piece of cloth worn over the shoulders, saying, “Receive, my beloved son, this scapular of thy order; it is the badge of my confraternity, a privilege for thee and for all Carmelites. Whoever dies clothed in this shall not suffer eternal fire.”\nHistorians question the details, but the devotion to the scapular grew. The Church teaches it is a sacramental: a sign of consecration to Mary and a reminder to live the Gospel. It is not a magic charm. He died in 1265.",
  script=dict(
    open="Hold up some items: holy water, a rosary, a medal, a candle. Ask, “Are these sacraments? What are they? Why do Catholics use them?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Sacramentals are ‘sacred signs which bear a certain resemblance to the sacraments, and by which spiritual effects are signified and obtained through the intercession of the Church.’ (CCC 1667) Unlike the sacraments, they were instituted by the Church, not Christ; they don’t confer sanctifying grace ex opere operato, but prepare us to receive it and dispose us to cooperate. Their effect depends on the faith of the user. Types: blessings (of persons, meals, objects, places), consecrations and dedications, exorcisms. Objects: holy water, blessed salt, ashes, palms, candles, rosaries, medals, scapulars, crucifixes, statues, icons, relics. Actions: Sign of the Cross, genuflection, processions, pilgrimages.”", "Say: “Why do we use them? We are embodied, and God used material things in the Incarnation. They remind us of God’s presence and sanctify the daily life. Holy water recalls Baptism. Ashes: we are dust. Candles: Christ the Light. Incense: prayer rising. They can be misused: superstition, treating them as lucky charms. The Church guards against both neglect and abuse. A blessing is a prayer calling God’s favor. The ordinary ministers are priests and deacons, but laypeople can give some blessings (parents blessing children). The Roman Ritual contains the Book of Blessings. Exorcism is a particular kind of sacramental for people possessed by demons; a major exorcism is allowed only by a priest with the bishop’s permission.”", "Say: “Popular piety. The Church values devotions as a way the faith takes root in cultures: the Rosary, Stations of the Cross, novenas, Eucharistic Adoration, Benediction, Divine Mercy chaplet, litanies, pilgrimages (Lourdes, Fatima, Guadalupe, Walsingham, Compostela), processions (Corpus Christi, Good Friday), shrines, Marian apparitions, the Sacred Heart, first Fridays, May crowning, and parish festivals. The Directory on Popular Piety (2001) says they must be in harmony with the liturgy, which is the source and summit. Devotions are optional; the liturgy has priority. They should be Christ-centered, rooted in Scripture and the Church’s teaching. The brown scapular reminds the wearer to follow Mary’s example, not a guarantee of salvation without conversion.”"],
    ask=["What is a sacramental? (A sacred sign instituted by the Church that prepares us for grace.)", "How do sacramentals differ from sacraments? (They don’t work ex opere operato; their effect depends on faith.)", "How should popular devotions relate to liturgy? (In harmony with it; liturgy has priority.)"],
    practice=["Make a three-column table: Sacraments / Sacramentals / Devotions, with ten examples.", "For each sacramental, write the sacrament it recalls.", "Identify one devotion you want to practice more.", "Write a short guide on how to use sacramentals rightly and avoid superstition."],
    close="Bless each other with holy water. Pray a Hail Mary. Send home: “Use a sacramental with intention this week.”")),

'11-10-2': dict(
  saint="Odilo of Cluny: the abbot who started the Commemoration of All Souls.",
  tryit=["Explain purgatory from Scripture and reason.", "Explain what an indulgence is and is not.", "List the conditions for a plenary indulgence."],
  saintStory="Odilo was born around 962 in the Auvergne in France to a noble family. He joined the abbey of Cluny, and became its abbot in 994, guiding it for over fifty years. Cluny at that time was the greatest monastery in Europe, with hundreds of daughter houses.\nOdilo was known for gentleness and generosity. During the famine of 1033 he used the monastery’s treasures to feed the starving and sold sacred vessels to help them. He promoted the “Peace of God” movement, which tried to protect civilians and clergy from violence. He also helped buy back captives.\nAround 998, he established that the monks of Cluny should pray and offer Mass for all the faithful departed on November 2, the day after All Saints. The custom spread to the whole Church and became the Commemoration of All Souls. He died in 1049, in his eighties, on a journey. He showed that love for the dead is part of love for the living.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “Why do Catholics pray for the dead? Where do you think a person goes who dies loving God but is not yet perfect?” Take answers.",
    teach=["Say: “Purgatory is the final purification of those who die in God’s grace and friendship but are still imperfectly purified. They are assured of heaven, but must undergo purification to achieve the holiness needed to enter. The Catechism: CCC 1030-1032. The doctrine was defined at the Councils of Lyon II (1274), Florence (1439) and Trent (1563). Scriptural roots: 2 Maccabees 12:41-46 (Judas Maccabeus prays for the dead and offers sacrifice for their sins); 1 Corinthians 3:11-15 (a person’s work tested ‘as through fire’); Matthew 12:32 (sin forgiven ‘in the age to come’); Revelation 21:27 (nothing unclean shall enter heaven). The Fathers, including Augustine, Monica’s request, and inscriptions in the catacombs, show prayers for the dead from the earliest times.”", "Say: “Purgatory is not ‘second chance’ or a mini-hell. It is a process of love: the soul’s purification, a pain of longing. Catherine of Genoa described it as the soul so full of desire to be with God that it wants to be cleansed. The ‘fire’ is the fire of love. The Church doesn’t define the details (location, time, nature of the fire). We can help the souls in purgatory by prayer, Mass, almsgiving and indulgences. November is the month of the Holy Souls. This is the communion of saints: the Church on earth helps the Church suffering.”", "Say: “Indulgences. After sin is forgiven in Confession, there remains temporal punishment: the harm done to oneself and others, and the need for purification. An indulgence is ‘a remission before God of the temporal punishment due to sins whose guilt has already been forgiven, which the faithful Christian who is duly disposed gains under certain prescribed conditions through the help of the Church.’ (CCC 1471) It draws on the treasury of merits of Christ and the saints. Partial indulgences remit part; plenary indulgences remit all. Conditions for a plenary: being in a state of grace, detachment from all sin including venial, sacramental Confession (within about 20 days before or after), Communion, prayer for the Pope’s intentions, and the indulgenced work (such as a Eucharistic adoration of half an hour, praying the Rosary in a church or family, Stations of the Cross, reading Scripture for half an hour). An indulgence can be applied to oneself or to the dead. Abuses: the sale of indulgences in the 1500s, one trigger of the Reformation; Pius V in 1567 abolished indulgences linked to money. Indulgences can’t be bought.”"],
    ask=["What is purgatory? (Final purification of those who die in grace but imperfectly purified.)", "What is an indulgence? (Remission of temporal punishment for sins already forgiven.)", "Can indulgences be bought? (No; selling them was abolished.)"],
    practice=["Make a chart: purgatory (Scripture, Fathers, councils, what it is and is not).", "List the conditions for a plenary indulgence and an act you could do.", "Write a prayer for the dead, naming someone.", "Plan to go to Mass on All Souls’ Day."],
    close="Pray the Eternal Rest prayer. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Pray for a deceased relative by name.”")),

'11-10-3': dict(
  saint="Macrina the Younger: a sister and teacher whose dying prayer her brother recorded.",
  tryit=["Map the stages of the Christian funeral.", "Match each to a meaning.", "Write a prayer you would want at your funeral."],
  saintStory="Macrina was born around 327 in Cappadocia, the oldest of ten children. Her family included Basil the Great, Gregory of Nyssa and Peter of Sebaste. She was engaged young, but when her fiancé died, she decided to remain unmarried, consecrated to God. She helped raise her younger brothers, including Basil, and taught them. When her father died, she persuaded her mother Emmelia to turn their estate into a monastic community where women and men lived simply, sharing work and prayer.\nShe supported Basil’s turn to monastic life. In 379, her brother Gregory visited her as she was dying. He wrote about her in The Life of Macrina. She lay on the floor on a rough board, with her last strength. She prayed in thanksgiving: “You, Lord, have freed us from the fear of death. You have made the end of this life the beginning of true life for us.”\nShe made the Sign of the Cross, whispered a final prayer, and died. Gregory describes preparing her body and her funeral. She was buried beside her parents. Her story shows a Christian death.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask, “How do other cultures treat the dead? What do you think a funeral is for?” Take answers. “The Church has a rich tradition. It teaches us how to grieve with hope.”",
    teach=["Say: “Christians face death with hope, but also sorrow. The Catechism: ‘Death is the end of man’s earthly pilgrimage… Death is transformed by Christ.’ Jesus wept at Lazarus’s tomb. Paul writes: ‘We would not have you ignorant, brethren, concerning those who are asleep, that you may not grieve as others do who have no hope.’ (1 Thessalonians 4:13) The Church’s funeral rites express faith in the Resurrection, pray for the deceased, and comfort the living. The Order of Christian Funerals has three stages. Vigil (wake): prayers, the Liturgy of the Word, the family gathers, eulogies may be given here. Funeral Liturgy: usually a Mass, with the body present; the casket is sprinkled with holy water (a baptismal reminder) and covered with a white pall; the Easter candle; incense; the homily preaches hope, not a eulogy; the Final Commendation and Farewell.”", "Say: “Committal: the rite at the grave or place of final disposition, with prayers asking for the deceased’s resurrection. The colors: white or purple; black is allowed. The funeral Mass uses prayers for the dead: ‘Eternal rest grant unto them, O Lord, and let perpetual light shine upon them.’ Burial in the ground is the preferred practice; the Church also permits cremation (Ad Resurgendum cum Christo, 2016), provided it is not chosen for reasons contrary to Christian faith. The ashes should be buried or entombed in a cemetery or sacred place, not scattered or kept at home, and not divided. Reasons: reverence for the body, a temple of the Spirit, destined for resurrection. Funeral rites are denied only to manifest apostates, or those who publicly refuse them, and in doubtful cases the pastor decides.”", "Say: “Support for the dying and the grieving. Before death: Anointing, Viaticum (Communion as ‘food for the journey’), the Commendation of the Dying, and prayers (the Litany, the Rosary). After: prayers for the deceased, Masses, visiting the grieving, preparing meals, listening. Grief is natural and takes time. The Church offers bereavement ministries. It encourages remembering the dead in November, visiting cemeteries, and praying. Macrina’s death shows trust: Christ has conquered death. Memento mori: remembering death helps us live well (Psalm 90:12). Life’s last things: death, judgment, heaven, hell. We prepare by living in the state of grace, making our wishes known, and writing wills. Praying the Hail Mary daily: ‘pray for us sinners, now and at the hour of our death.’”"],
    ask=["What are the three stages of the Christian funeral? (Vigil, funeral liturgy, committal.)", "What does the Church teach about cremation? (Permitted, but ashes should be buried or entombed, not scattered.)", "Why is the homily at a funeral not a eulogy? (It proclaims the hope of the Resurrection.)"],
    practice=["Make a timeline of a Catholic funeral with the rites and symbols: wake, procession, holy water, pall, Easter candle, incense, commendation, committal.", "Beside each, write its meaning and baptismal connection.", "Write the Scripture readings and hymns you would choose for your own funeral, and why.", "Write a short prayer for your family at your death."],
    close="Pray the Eternal Rest prayer and a Hail Mary. Sign of the Cross. Send home: “Visit a cemetery and pray for the dead.”")),
}
